

. Understand Your Attack Surface

Attack surface includes:

* **Network**: open ports, services, firewalls
* **Applications**: web apps, APIs, mobile apps
* **Endpoints**: laptops, servers, IoT devices
* **Users**: credentials, permissions, human behavior
* **Data**: databases, backups, logs, secrets

You can’t protect what you don’t know exists.



. Minimize Exposure (Most Important)

**Remove anything unnecessary**

* Disable unused services, ports, and protocols
* Uninstall unused software and plugins
* Remove old APIs and legacy systems
* Shut down test, dev, and staging systems exposed to the internet

> Less functionality = less risk

---

. Apply Strong Access Control

* **Least privilege**: users and services get only what they need
* Use **role-based access control (RBAC)**
* Enforce **multi-factor authentication (MFA)** everywhere possible
* Avoid shared accounts
* Rotate credentials and API keys regularly

---

. Harden Systems

* Secure OS and application configurations (CIS benchmarks)
* Disable default accounts and passwords
* Use secure headers (HSTS, CSP, etc.) for web apps
* Encrypt data **in transit** (TLS) and **at rest**

---

. Patch and Update Regularly

* Apply OS, firmware, and application updates promptly
* Patch known vulnerabilities (CVEs)
* Automate patch management where possible

Unpatched systems are one of the top attack vectors.

---

. Protect Network Boundaries

* Use firewalls and allow only required traffic
* Segment networks (don’t put everything on one flat network)
* Restrict admin access to internal or VPN-only networks
* Monitor inbound and outbound traffic

---

. Secure Applications & APIs

* Validate all user input (prevent SQLi, XSS, command injection)
* Use authentication and authorization on every API
* Rate-limit APIs to prevent abuse
* Hide internal errors and stack traces
* Perform code reviews and security testing

---

. Monitor and Detect

* Centralized logging and monitoring
* Intrusion detection / prevention systems (IDS/IPS)
* Alert on suspicious behavior (failed logins, privilege escalation)
* Regularly review logs

---

. Train Users (Human Attack Surface)

* Security awareness training
* Phishing simulations
* Clear password and device policies
* Encourage reporting suspicious activity

Humans are often the weakest link.

---

. Regularly Test Your Security

* Vulnerability scanning
* Penetration testing
* Red team / blue team exercises
* Attack surface discovery tools

Security is not “set and forget”.

---

### Simple Rule of Thumb

> **Reduce, Restrict, Patch, Monitor**
